WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.865
Use-after-free prevention in safe Rust
comes from the language model,

2
00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:06.918
but lifecycle-sensitive code still needs
testing and review.

3
00:00:06.998 --> 00:00:10.812
Tests should cover construction, ownership
transfer, cleanup, cancellation,

4
00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:14.313
retries, dropped handles, failed
initialization, and shutdown paths.

5
00:00:14.393 --> 00:00:17.702
These are the places where lifecycle
assumptions are easiest to miss.

6
00:00:17.782 --> 00:00:20.362
Drop-path review matters for
resource-owning types.

7
00:00:20.442 --> 00:00:23.078
What happens if initialization fails
halfway through?

8
00:00:23.158 --> 00:00:26.692
What happens if a handle is dropped while
background work is still pending?

9
00:00:26.772 --> 00:00:28.623
What happens during service shutdown?

10
00:00:28.703 --> 00:00:30.834
Does cleanup happen once, in the right
order,

11
00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:33.606
and without leaving callbacks or tasks
with stale state?

12
00:00:33.686 --> 00:00:37.164
Code review should focus on unsafe blocks,
FFI, callbacks, raw pointers,

13
00:00:37.244 --> 00:00:40.441
shared ownership, `Weak` upgrade behavior,
interior mutability,

14
00:00:40.521 --> 00:00:43.381
async task boundaries, and custom `Drop`
implementations.

15
00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:47.275
Reviewers should ask what owns each
resource, how long references remain valid,

16
00:00:47.355 --> 00:00:49.094
and what prevents use after cleanup.

17
00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:52.259
Tooling concepts such as Miri and
sanitizers can support review,

18
00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:54.021
especially near unsafe or FFI code.

19
00:00:54.101 --> 00:00:56.625
They do not replace design, documentation,
or tests,

20
00:00:56.705 --> 00:00:59.790
but they can provide extra confidence
around boundary behavior.

21
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.852
Dependency review also matters when
external crates manage lifetimes, callbacks,

22
00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:05.110
or low-level resources.

23
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.275
Regression tests are important when a
lifetime bug, cleanup bug,

24
00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:10.318
or stale-reference assumption is fixed.

25
00:01:10.398 --> 00:01:12.753
The fix should not remain only in a review
comment.

26
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:16.591
Capture the lifecycle edge in a test where
practical, update safety contracts,

27
00:01:16.671 --> 00:01:19.700
and make sure future refactors preserve
the intended behavior.
